<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bourgeoisie</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as bourgeois</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The bourgeoisie is a social class of business owners, merchants and wealthy people that emerged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-middle-ages">Late Middle Ages</a> as a middle class between the peasantry and the aristocracy. The word comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> borgeis, 'town dweller', from bourg, 'market town': the medieval bourgeois were the craftsmen, artisans and merchants of the towns, which grew into cities of commerce and crafts from the 11th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>, the American War of Independence and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> were partly motivated by their wish to be rid of feudal and royal limits on property and trade. In Marxist theory it owns the means of production and exploits the labour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie.jpg" alt="Bourgeoisie" width="330" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Béraud · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Béraud_-_La_Sortie_du_Bourgeois.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For it were a petty <b>bourgeoisie</b> of lawyers such as Moreno, Castelli, Belgrano and Paso, popular priests, doctors and common workers like Domingo French and Antonio Beruti, and a new merchant bourgeoisie of criollo and British houses whose trade with other countries, long treated as smuggling, had been…</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1926 the Communist Party felt secure enough to act against traditional authorities without the Jadids, who were denounced as the mouthpiece of the local <b>bourgeoisie</b> and counterrevolutionary agents to be stripped of their jobs, arrested and executed if necessary.</blockquote><a href="/movement/jadidism">Jadidism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Communists denounced it as a betrayal of working-class ideals by an indifferent <b>bourgeoisie</b>, and the view of 1848 as a bourgeois revolution is also common in non-Marxist scholarship.</blockquote><a href="/movement/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848 · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Communists hold that the present order stems from capitalism, which divides society into two classes: the proletariat, the majority, who must sell their labour power to survive, and the <b>bourgeoisie</b>, a minority that profits from owning the means of production.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>Under capitalism the <b>bourgeoisie</b>, who own the means of production, face the proletariat, who must sell their labour power.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/marxism">Marxism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>A vanguard party, organised through democratic centralism, seizes power on behalf of the proletariat and founds a one-party state, which controls the means of production and suppresses the <b>bourgeoisie</b> and counter-revolution, in order to pave the way for a classless and stateless communist society.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism · Core Ideas</a></li></ul></section>
